+<!--
+Sync Impact Report
+==================
+Version change: (none) → 1.0.0
+Rationale: Initial ratification. The template shipped with Spec Kit contained only
+placeholder tokens; this is the first concrete constitution for this repository, so the
+version starts at 1.0.0 rather than being a bump of a prior version.
+
+Modified principles: none (all five principles newly authored)
+Added sections:
+  - Core Principles (I. Library-First, Then CLI / II. Existing Conventions Are Law /
+    III. Test Coverage Is Non-Negotiable / IV. Typed Python, Built Frontend /
+    V. Dependency Restraint)
+  - Additional Constraints: Technology & Packaging
+  - Development Workflow & Quality Gates
+  - Governance
+Removed sections: none
+Deferred TODOs: none — RATIFICATION_DATE is set to the date this constitution was first
+authored for this repository (2026-07-31), which is accurate for this document even though
+the underlying open-source project predates it.
+-->
+
+# sqlite-utils Contribution Constitution
+
+This constitution governs work performed on the `sqlite-utils` repository (simonw/sqlite-utils)
+by agents and contributors following the Spec Kit workflow. `sqlite-utils` is a mature,
+widely-deployed open-source CLI tool and Python library. Every rule below exists because this
+is *existing* software with *existing* users — not a greenfield project.
+
+## Core Principles
+
+### I. Library-First, Then CLI
+
+Every feature MUST be implemented as a Python library API first, and only then exposed through
+the `sqlite-utils` command-line interface. The CLI layer MUST contain argument parsing, output
+formatting, and exit-code selection — nothing else. Business logic MUST live in importable
+modules under `sqlite_utils/` so it is usable, testable, and documentable without invoking a
+subprocess.
+
+Rationale: the project's own README states "Most of the functionality is available as either a
+Python API or through the `sqlite-utils` command-line tool." A feature that is CLI-only breaks
+that promise, and CLI-only logic is excluded from `mypy` by the repository's own `mypy.ini`
+(`[mypy-sqlite_utils.cli] ignore_errors = True`), so it would silently escape type checking.
+
+### II. Existing Conventions Are Law
+
+Before writing any code, the existing conventions of `db.py`, `cli.py`, `tests/`, and `docs/`
+MUST be read and followed. This includes: exception classes and where they are raised, how
+`click` commands are registered and how their options are named, `@pytest.fixture` and
+`CliRunner` usage patterns in `tests/`, reStructuredText section-label conventions
+(`.. _cli_<topic>:`) in `docs/`, and `cog` blocks in `docs/cli-reference.rst`.
+
+Inventing a parallel convention — a second error hierarchy, a different option-naming style, a
+new test-harness idiom — is a defect even when the code works. When repository convention and
+personal preference conflict, repository convention wins.
+
+Rationale: this is an upstream contribution. Code that does not look like the surrounding code
+will not be merged, and divergent conventions compound maintenance cost for every future reader.
+
+### III. Test Coverage Is Non-Negotiable
+
+The pre-existing test suite MUST pass in full, unchanged, with zero regressions. Existing tests
+MUST NOT be weakened, skipped, or rewritten to accommodate new code.
+
+New behaviour MUST be covered by new tests placed in the repository's existing `tests/` layout,
+covering at minimum: the success path, each distinct failure mode, boundary/empty inputs, and
+every documented exit code. Frontend logic MUST be covered by `vitest` running in a DOM
+environment. Tests MUST run offline — no test may require network access.
+
+Rationale: `sqlite-utils` is depended upon by production data pipelines. A regression here is
+someone's broken ETL job at 3am.
+
+### IV. Typed Python, Built Frontend
+
+All new Python code MUST carry complete type annotations and MUST pass `mypy` under the
+repository's existing `mypy.ini` with no new ignores and no lowering of existing strictness.
+New Python code MUST also pass `black --check` and `flake8`.
+
+All browser-facing code MUST be authored in TypeScript and compiled by a real build pipeline.
+`tsc --noEmit` MUST be clean. Generating HTML or JavaScript by string-concatenation inside
+Python is FORBIDDEN; Python's only role in report rendering is to inject serialized data into a
+pre-built artifact. Report UI MUST be structured as "data in, DOM out" so its logic is testable
+without a browser-driving harness.
+
+Rationale: the repository already ships `py.typed` and runs `mypy` in CI; new untyped code would
+degrade a guarantee downstream users rely on. The frontend rule exists because a 3000-line
+inlined template string is unreviewable and unmaintainable — nobody dares edit it.
+
+### V. Dependency Restraint
+
+Every new runtime dependency added to `[project.dependencies]` MUST be justified in writing in
+the feature's decision record, including what it provides and why a standard-library or
+vendored-asset alternative is insufficient. Preference order is: (1) standard library,
+(2) a dependency the project already has, (3) a build-time-only dev dependency, (4) a new
+runtime dependency as a last resort.
+
+Generated artifacts MUST be self-contained: a produced HTML report MUST function with no
+network access whatsoever — no CDN scripts, no remote stylesheets, no external fonts, no remote
+images, no telemetry.
+
+Rationale: `sqlite-utils` is installed into constrained and air-gapped environments. Each
+runtime dependency is imposed on every user of the library, including those who never touch the
+new feature.
+
+## Additional Constraints: Technology & Packaging
+
+- **Python**: `>=3.10` (per `pyproject.toml`). New code MUST NOT use syntax or standard-library
+  APIs unavailable on 3.10.
+- **Environment**: local development uses `uv`. Tests run via `uv run pytest`; linters via the
+  `Justfile` recipes (`just lint`).
+- **Frontend toolchain**: TypeScript with `vite` or `esbuild`, tested with `vitest` in a DOM
+  environment (`jsdom`/`happy-dom`). Frontend toolchain packages are development-only and MUST
+  NOT become Python runtime dependencies.
+- **Build artifacts**: the compiled frontend bundle MUST be committed to the repository and
+  shipped inside the Python package (via `[tool.setuptools.package-data]`), so that installing
+  from PyPI requires no Node.js toolchain at install time or runtime.
+- **Documentation**: user-facing changes MUST be documented in `docs/` using reStructuredText,
+  and any new CLI command MUST appear in `docs/cli.rst` (enforced by
+  `tests/test_docs.py::test_commands_are_documented`) and in the `cog`-generated
+  `docs/cli-reference.rst`.
+
+## Development Workflow & Quality Gates
+
+Work proceeds through the Spec Kit phases in order — constitution, specify, clarify, plan,
+tasks, analyze, implement — with no phase skipped, merged, or reordered. Implementation code
+MUST NOT be written before the `tasks` phase has produced an approved task list.
+
+Product and business decisions (what counts as a violation, what a user is trying to learn from
+a report, what limits are acceptable to the business) MUST be escalated to the requesting
+stakeholder rather than guessed. Technical decisions (library choice, module layout, data
+structures, algorithms) are made by the implementer and MUST be recorded in the feature's
+decision record.
+
+A change is complete only when ALL of the following pass:
+
+1. `uv run pytest` — full suite, zero failures, no reduction in the pre-existing pass count.
+2. `uv run mypy sqlite_utils tests` — clean.
+3. `uv run black . --check` and `uv run flake8` — clean.
+4. `npx tsc --noEmit` — clean, and `vitest run` — all frontend tests pass.
+5. Documentation updated and `cog --check` satisfied.
+6. Generated report artifacts verified to contain no external references.
+
+Committing to a feature branch locally is expected. Pushing to any remote is FORBIDDEN unless
+explicitly requested.
+
+## Governance
+
+This constitution supersedes ad-hoc preferences and default agent behaviour for work in this
+repository. Where this constitution and an upstream repository convention appear to conflict,
+the upstream convention governs the code and this document MUST be amended to record the
+correction.
+
+**Amendment procedure**: amendments are made by editing `.specify/memory/constitution.md`,
+recording the change in the Sync Impact Report comment at the top of the file, and bumping the
+version. Amendments that arise mid-feature MUST be applied before implementation continues.
+
+**Versioning policy**: semantic versioning.
+- MAJOR — a principle is removed, or redefined in a way that invalidates prior compliant work.
+- MINOR — a principle or governance section is added, or existing guidance is materially expanded.
+- PATCH — clarification, wording, or typo fixes that do not change what is required.
+
+**Compliance review**: the `speckit-analyze` phase MUST check every artifact against these
+principles before implementation begins, and the final report MUST state the result of each of
+the six quality gates listed above with real command output — not assertions.
+
+**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Ratified**: 2026-07-31 | **Last Amended**: 2026-07-31
diff --git a/.specify/scripts/bash/check-prerequisites.sh b/.specify/scripts/bash/check-prerequisites.sh
