+.. _cli_validate:
+
+Validating data against a JSON Schema
+=====================================
+
+The ``sqlite-utils validate`` command checks the contents of a table against a `JSON Schema <https://json-schema.org/>`__ document, and reports the rows that do not conform.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db events schema.json
+
+.. code-block:: output
+
+    events: 5 violation(s) in 4 of 4 row(s)
+      type-coercible: 2
+      type-invalid: 2
+      enum: 1
+
+The schema describes what a single row should look like. Its ``properties`` correspond to the columns of the table:
+
+.. code-block:: json
+
+    {
+        "type": "object",
+        "properties": {
+            "id": {"type": "integer"},
+            "age": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 0},
+            "status": {"type": "string", "enum": ["active", "inactive"]},
+            "email": {"type": ["string", "null"], "format": "email"}
+        },
+        "required": ["id", "status"]
+    }
+
+.. _cli_validate_exit_codes:
+
+Exit codes
+----------
+
+The command uses distinct exit codes so it can be used to gate a script or a data pipeline:
+
+==========  ==================================================================
+Exit code   Meaning
+==========  ==================================================================
+``0``       The table passed - no violations were found
+``1``       Validation ran and found one or more violations
+``2``       The check could not run: the table does not exist, or the schema
+            file is missing, unparsable, or uses an unsupported keyword
+==========  ==================================================================
+
+Exit code ``2`` is deliberately distinct from ``1``: a broken schema file must never be mistaken for clean data.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    if ! sqlite-utils validate data.db events schema.json; then
+        echo "Data quality check failed"
+    fi
+
+.. _cli_validate_types:
+
+How values are compared
+-----------------------
+
+Values are compared against the declared type using their SQLite storage class, **strictly by default**. Because a CSV import may leave a column stored as text, a value such as ``"42"`` in a column declared as ``integer`` is reported - but as a separate kind of violation from a value such as ``"abc"``:
+
+* ``type-coercible`` - the value could be read as the declared type, but is not stored as it. Usually means the column was never typed properly.
+* ``type-invalid`` - the value cannot be read as the declared type at all.
+
+Filtering these apart makes it easy to distinguish "this column needs converting" from "this row contains junk". Use ``--lenient-types`` to accept coercible values:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db events schema.json --lenient-types
+
+A SQLite ``null`` is treated as JSON ``null``. It does not trigger ``required`` - the column exists either way - but it is rejected by ``type`` unless the schema allows it explicitly:
+
+.. code-block:: json
+
+    {"type": ["string", "null"]}
+
+The empty string is a valid string and is not the same as ``null``.
+
+Columns that the schema does not mention are governed by the schema's own ``additionalProperties`` keyword, and are ignored when it is not set.
+
+.. _cli_validate_supported:
+
+Supported keywords
+------------------
+
+``type`` (including unions such as ``["string", "null"]``), ``properties``, ``required``, ``enum``, ``const``, ``minimum``, ``maximum``, ``exclusiveMinimum``, ``exclusiveMaximum``, ``multipleOf``, ``minLength``, ``maxLength``, ``pattern``, ``format`` and ``additionalProperties``.
+
+Supported ``format`` values are ``date``, ``date-time``, ``time``, ``email``, ``uri`` and ``ipv4``.
+
+Any other keyword - including ``$ref``, ``allOf``, ``anyOf``, ``oneOf`` and ``if``/``then``/``else`` - causes the command to stop with exit code ``2`` rather than validate the table while ignoring it. Reporting a table as valid when part of the schema was skipped would be a false assurance.
+
+.. _cli_validate_reports:
+
+Reports
+-------
+
+Use ``--json`` to emit the full result as a single JSON document, suitable for storing in a pipeline:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db events schema.json --json
+
+Use ``--html`` to write a self-contained interactive report that can be opened directly in a browser and shared with people who do not use the command line:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db events schema.json --html report.html
+
+The HTML file embeds everything it needs. It makes no network requests and works with no connectivity. The report can be filtered by column and by violation kind, searched, and sorted, and each violation expands to show what was expected against what was found.
+
+By default the report shows only the value of the offending column. Pass ``--full-row`` to include every column of each failing row.
+
+.. _cli_validate_large:
+
+Large tables
+------------
+
+Two independent limits keep the command usable on very large tables:
+
+* ``--max-violations N`` caps how many individual violations are listed, defaulting to 1000. The summary counts remain true totals, so a truncated report still reports the real scale of the problem.
+* ``--scan-limit N`` stops after examining the first N rows, for a fast spot-check.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db events schema.json --scan-limit 1000
+
+Both are reported separately, so "only 1000 rows were checked" is never confused with "only 1000 problems were listed".
+
+.. note::
+    In Python: :ref:`validate_table() <python_api_validate>`  CLI reference: :ref:`sqlite-utils validate <cli_ref_validate>`
+
