+.. _python_api_validate:
+
+Validating a table against a JSON Schema
+========================================
+
+The ``sqlite_utils.validate`` module checks the contents of a table against a
+`JSON Schema <https://json-schema.org/>`__ document. It powers the
+:ref:`validate <cli_validate>` CLI command, and can be used directly:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from sqlite_utils import Database
+    from sqlite_utils.validate import validate_table
+
+    db = Database("data.db")
+    result = validate_table(db, "events", {
+        "type": "object",
+        "properties": {
+            "id": {"type": "integer"},
+            "status": {"type": "string", "enum": ["active", "inactive"]},
+        },
+        "required": ["id"],
+    })
+
+    if not result.ok:
+        print(result.summary.total_violations, "problems")
+        for violation in result.violations:
+            print(violation.row_id, violation.column, violation.kind, violation.message)
+
+``validate_table()`` takes the following arguments:
+
+``db`` - Database
+    The database containing the table.
+
+``table`` - string
+    The name of the table to validate. Raises ``sqlite_utils.db.NoTable`` if it
+    does not exist.
+
+``schema`` - dictionary or CompiledSchema
+    The JSON Schema to validate against. Raises
+    ``sqlite_utils.validate.SchemaError`` if the schema uses a keyword this
+    module does not support - it will never validate a table while ignoring part
+    of the schema.
+
+``lenient_types`` - boolean
+    Accept values that could be read as the declared type. Defaults to ``False``.
+
+``max_violations`` - integer
+    How many individual violations to retain. Defaults to 1000. Summary counts
+    remain true totals when this limit is applied.
+
+``scan_limit`` - integer or None
+    Examine at most this many rows. Independent of ``max_violations``.
+
+``include_full_row`` - boolean
+    Include every column of a failing row in each violation. Defaults to
+    ``False``, so only the offending column's value is captured.
+
+It returns a ``ValidationResult`` with an ``ok`` property, a ``summary`` and a
+list of ``violations``. Call ``result.to_dict()`` to get a JSON-serializable
+structure.
+
+To render a self-contained HTML report from a result:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from sqlite_utils.validate import build_html_report
+
+    open("report.html", "w").write(build_html_report(result))
+
+The returned document embeds its own styles, script and data. It makes no
+network requests and contains no filesystem paths.
+
+.. note::
+    In the CLI: :ref:`sqlite-utils validate <cli_validate>`
